Phase 0 (engine.go, node.go, runtime.go, builtin.go): no source lines copied
verbatim — interfaces and control flow reimplemented against Go idioms
already used elsewhere in this SDK (see pkg/workflow).

Phase 1 (nodes/http.go): the SSRF blocklist (blockedNetworks/blockedHosts in
validateURLForSSRF) is a close port of m9m's internal/nodes/http SSRF guard —
the same CIDR ranges and blocked hostnames, translated to Go idiom but not
independently re-derived. Everything else in nodes/ (logic.go, data.go,
wait.go, expr/pool.go) is an independent reimplementation informed by
reading m9m's transform/expressions packages, not a port.

Phase 2 (nodes/database/): independent reimplementations, not ports — the
operation surface (executeQuery/insert/update/delete for SQL,
get/set/del/keys/hget/hset/lpush/rpush for Redis, find/insertOne/updateOne/
deleteOne for MongoDB, search/index/get/delete for Elasticsearch) mirrors
m9m's node design, but the transport differs deliberately: m9m's Redis and
MongoDB nodes only work through an HTTP REST proxy (Upstash-style / MongoDB
Atlas Data API), which a plain self-hosted deployment doesn't expose — this
package uses each service's native Go driver instead so it works against any
standard deployment. sqlite.go also deliberately uses modernc.org/sqlite
(pure Go) instead of m9m's mattn/go-sqlite3 (cgo), so importing this package
never forces a C toolchain onto a consumer.
